// A boundary row is settled only by an exact nongeneric realization. This
// provider machine is generic over one runtime Value binder, so the checked
// adapter selected for `Indexed::read` cannot settle the row even though the
// declaration is otherwise well formed.
//
// The carrier-era spelling of the same rule rejected the open operand directly
// ("open operands do not close a requirement instance"). A direct open
// requirement application now checks clean inside a generic machine (measured:
// `machine probe<Count: u64>() reaches Indexed { Indexed<Count>::read() }`
// exits 0), because no program entry reaches it and reachability closes the
// operand before a row is demanded; the surviving enforcement is here, on the
// realization that would settle the row.

public boundary trait Indexed<const Count: u64> { machine read() -> i32 reaches; }
data BadProvider {}
machine BadProvider::read<Count: u64>() -> i32 satisfies Indexed<Count>::read { 0 }
